% The customs background (lpsPlus docs/strategy/FactsBackground.md §6.1):
% the outside background. A shop listing can offer a good of a chapter that
% the model does not have: a lamp, a pen, a speaker, a chair. Then the
% model must not give a code of a modelled chapter as if it were sure. This
% program reads the words of a listing that show such a good. It does not
% exclude the good. It raises a claim: "the good is claimed to belong to
% lighting fittings of heading 9405". A claim is an open question for the
% office (gri.le, "Headings outside the model"). While the office has not
% rejected the claim, the model's answer is conditional on that judgment.
% The listing's words are evidence, not proof.
%
% The model has Chapters 39, 42, 61, 62, 63, 64, 67, 71, 91 and 95. This
% program never claims one of them.
%
% Each word cites a public text:
%   (a) the exclusion notes of the modelled chapters, which give examples of
%       the goods that they send to other chapters: Chapter 39 note 2,
%       Section XI note 1, Chapter 42 note 2, Chapter 64 note 1, Chapter 67
%       note 1, Chapter 71 notes 3 and 11. The note's own text is the citation;
%   (b) the headings of the other chapters (84, 85, 90, 94, 96, 65, 66),
%       and US Customs rulings that name a good of those headings;
%   (c) the description of the listing fields
%       (sources/listings/marketplace_fields_other_products.txt): the fields
%       of a powered product ("Power Source: Battery", "Electronic: Yes",
%       "Voltage: 12 V") and the shop's categories ("Consumer Electronics",
%       "Lights & Lighting", "Furniture").
%
% Four rules raise a claim:
%   claim_by_name      the name of the good is a word of this program;
%   claim_by_field     a field that names the product (Item Type, Type,
%                      Category, Product Type, Use, Application) reads a word
%                      of this program;
%   claim_by_category  a shop category of the good is a category of goods of
%                      another chapter;
%   claim_by_power_field, claim_by_power_value
%                      a field of a powered product shows that the good uses
%                      electricity: a claim to the machinery of Section XVI.
%
% How the rules keep false claims few:
%   - The words are whole names. "pen" raises a claim; "pen holder" does
%     not, because no word of this program is "pen holder".
%   - A category and a power field are weak evidence. They raise no claim
%     when an earlier background program gave the good a kind (a garment, a
%     shoe, a bag, a piece of jewellery: their chapters are modelled), or
%     when the shop's category is one of toys or of watches (Section XVI
%     note 1 sends toys to Chapter 95 and watches to Chapter 91, and the
%     model will have both). A category also raises no claim when the uses
%     background gave the good a principal use (a phone case of plastics in
%     "Consumer Electronics" is an article of Chapter 39, HQ H284146).
%   - A power field raises no claim when a word or a category of the good
%     already raises one (a battery LED light string is a lamp of 9405, NY
%     N239763, not a machine of Section XVI).
%
% How the rules treat goods that the model classifies now:
%   - A phone case of plastics: no claim, unless the listing says that it
%     has a battery ("Is Electric: Lithium Battery"). A case with a battery
%     is a power bank, an accumulator of heading 8507 (NY N265396), so the
%     claim is right.
%   - A plastic part of a toy with batteries: the shop's category "Toys &
%     Hobbies" stops the power-field claim. A listing that gives no category
%     gets the claim: a false claim, which the office rejects.
%   - A battery-powered garment (a heated jacket or pullover): the garments
%     background gives the kind, so no claim. Chapter 85 note 1(a) excludes
%     electrically warmed clothing, and NY N287133 puts a heated pullover in
%     6110.
%   - Light-up shoes: the footwear background gives the kind, so no claim
%     (NY N341830 puts light-up shoes in 6402).
%   - A bag with a USB port: the footwear background gives the kind, so no
%     claim.
%
% Left out, because the word does not decide the chapter or because no
% saved text supports it:
%   - "bedding", "pillow", "cushion" (heading 9404 or bed linen of 6302,
%     Chapter 63, which the model will have);
%   - "buckle", "eyelet", "laces" (Chapter 64 note 2 sends them to "their
%     appropriate headings": of plastics they stay in Chapter 39);
%   - "Certification: CE,FCC,RoHS": sellers give it to goods of every kind;
%   - "Tools", "Home Improvement", "Beauty & Health", "Sports &
%     Entertainment", "Office & School Supplies" as categories: they hold
%     goods of modelled chapters too;
%   - "machine", "appliance", "device" as names: no listing names a good
%     by that word alone;
%   - "eyewear", "zipper", "stool", "smartwatch": no saved text.
%
% Used only to read facts from a text (language reference §14.5).
%
% DISCLAIMER. This program is an example of Logical English. It is provided
% "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or implied, including any
% warranty that it is accurate, complete, up to date or fit for a particular
% purpose. It is not an official reading of the tariff or of the rulings it
% cites, it is not a binding classification ruling, and it is not legal or
% customs advice. Its answers may be wrong: do not rely on them for a real
% import, export or declaration; consult the official texts, the customs
% authority and a qualified professional. Its authors accept no liability for
% any loss or damage arising from its use.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a thing* is called *a name*; undefined.
    the field *a field* of *a thing* reads *a value*; undefined.
    *a thing* is in the shop category *a category*.
    *a name* is a word for an article of *a kind*.
    *a field* is a field that names the product.
    *a category* is a shop category of articles of *a kind*.
    *a category* is a shop category of goods of a modelled chapter.
    *a field* reading *a value* shows a powered product.
    *a field* is a field of a powered product.
    *a value* is a word for no value.
    *a good* has a word of goods of another chapter.
    the kind of *a good* is *a kind*.
    the principal use of *a good* is *a use*.
    *a good* is claimed to belong to *a kind*.

